[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Packaging Spring Boot Applications: Executable JARs and WARs"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spring Boot simplifies the process of packaging applications for deployment. It primarily supports two packaging formats: executable JARs (Java Archive) and WARs (Web Application Archive). This tutorial will guide you through creating both types of packages."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Executable JARs"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Executable JARs are self-contained archives that include the application code, dependencies, and an embedded servlet container (like Tomcat, Jetty, or Undertow). This makes them easy to deploy and run without requiring an external application server."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an Executable JAR with Maven"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Maven is a popular build automation tool for Java projects. To create an executable JAR, you need to configure the `spring-boot-maven-plugin` in your `pom.xml` file."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"```xml
<build>
  <plugins>
    <plugin>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    </plugin>
  </plugins>
</build>
```"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"This plugin provides the `spring-boot:repackage` goal, which repackages the JAR file to make it executable. By default, it searches for a `public static void main(String[] args)` method in your project to define the entry point."},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"To build the executable JAR, run the following Maven command in your project directory:"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"```bash
mvn clean install
```"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"After the build completes, you'll find the executable JAR file in the `target` directory. It will have a name similar to `your-application-name-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar`."},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can run the JAR file using the following command:"},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"```bash
java -jar your-application-name-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar
```"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating an Executable JAR with Gradle"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Gradle is another popular build automation tool. To create an executable JAR with Gradle, you need to configure the `org.springframework.boot` plugin in your `build.gradle` file."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"```groovy
plugins {
  id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.0.0' // Or your Spring Boot version
  id 'io.spring.dependency-management' version '1.0.11.RELEASE'
  id 'java'
}

dependencies {
  implementation('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-web')
  testImplementation('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test')
}

tasks.named('bootJar') {
  archiveFileName = 'my-app.jar'
}
```"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"This plugin automatically configures the necessary tasks to create an executable JAR.  The `bootJar` task is configured to rename the output jar file."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To build the executable JAR, run the following Gradle command in your project directory:"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"```bash
gradle clean build
```"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"After the build completes, you'll find the executable JAR file in the `build/libs` directory. It will have a name similar to `my-app.jar`."},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can run the JAR file using the following command:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"```bash
java -jar build/libs/my-app.jar
```"},{"order":23,"type":"heading","text":"WARs (Web Application Archives)"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"WARs are used to package web applications for deployment to a traditional servlet container like Tomcat or Jetty. Unlike executable JARs, WARs do not include an embedded server. They rely on the external container to provide the runtime environment."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a WAR with Maven"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a WAR file, you need to change the `packaging` element in your `pom.xml` file to `war`."},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"```xml
<packaging>war</packaging>
```"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Also, you might need to make your Spring Boot application class extend `SpringBootServletInitializer` and override the `configure` method. This is necessary for the servlet container to properly initialize your application."},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"```java
import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.builder.SpringApplicationBuilder;
import org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.support.SpringBootServletInitializer;

@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApplication extends SpringBootServletInitializer {

    @Override
    protected SpringApplicationBuilder configure(SpringApplicationBuilder application) {
        return application.sources(MyApplication.class);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApplication.class, args);
    }
}
```"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Ensure the `spring-boot-maven-plugin` is configured as shown in the Executable JAR section."},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"To build the WAR file, run the following Maven command:"},{"order":32,"type":"code","text":"```bash
mvn clean install
```"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"After the build completes, you'll find the WAR file in the `target` directory. It will have a name similar to `your-application-name-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.war`."},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can deploy this WAR file to a servlet container like Tomcat or Jetty."},{"order":35,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a WAR with Gradle"},{"order":36,"type":"paragraph","text":"To create a WAR file with Gradle, you need to change the `plugins` section and add `war` plugin, and also update the `build.gradle` file similar to the Maven approach by extending `SpringBootServletInitializer`."},{"order":37,"type":"code","text":"```groovy
plugins {
  id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.0.0' // Or your Spring Boot version
  id 'io.spring.dependency-management' version '1.0.11.RELEASE'
  id 'java'
  id 'war'
}

dependencies {
  implementation('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-web')
  providedRuntime('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-tomcat')
  testImplementation('org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test')
}

war {
  archiveFileName = 'my-app.war'
}

```"},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `providedRuntime` dependency is important. It tells Gradle that the servlet container will provide the Tomcat runtime, so it doesn't need to be included in the WAR file.  Also, the `war` task is configured to rename the output war file."},{"order":39,"type":"paragraph","text":"You also need to extend `SpringBootServletInitializer` as shown in the Maven WAR example."},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"To build the WAR file, run the following Gradle command:"},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"```bash
gradle clean build
```"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"After the build completes, you'll find the WAR file in the `build/libs` directory. It will have a name similar to `my-app.war`."},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can deploy this WAR file to a servlet container like Tomcat or Jetty."},{"order":44,"type":"heading","text":"Choosing Between JAR and WAR"},{"order":45,"type":"paragraph","text":"The choice between JAR and WAR depends on your deployment environment and requirements."},{"order":46,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Executable JARs when you want a self-contained application that is easy to deploy and run without an external application server. This is ideal for microservices and cloud-native applications."},{"order":47,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use WARs when you need to deploy your application to a traditional servlet container or when you have specific requirements for managing the servlet container configuration."},{"order":48,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":49,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial covered the basics of packaging Spring Boot applications as executable JARs and WARs. By understanding the differences between these formats and how to create them using Maven and Gradle, you can choose the best approach for your specific deployment needs."}]
